==Round of 16==

===Brazil vs Chile===
{{further|List of 2014 FIFA World Cup controversies#Brazil vs Chile}}
The two teams had met in 68 previous matches,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/statisticsandrecords/headtohead/team1=bra/team2=chi/code=-1/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100629204651/http://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/statisticsandrecords/headtohead/team1=bra/team2=chi/code=-1/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=29 June 2010 |title=Brazil – Chile |publisher=FIFA |access-date=28 June 2014 }}</ref> including three times in the FIFA World Cup knockout stage, all won by Brazil ([[1962 FIFA World Cup|1962]], semi-finals: 4–2; [[1998 FIFA World Cup|1998]], round of 16: [[1998 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Brazil vs Chile|4–1]]; [[2010 FIFA World Cup|2010]], round of 16: [[2010 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Brazil vs Chile|3–0]]).

Brazil opened the scoring when [[Thiago Silva]] flicked on a corner kick before [[David Luiz]] turned the ball into the net with his thigh. While replays suggested that Chilean defender [[Gonzalo Jara (footballer, born 1985)|Gonzalo Jara]] may have had the last touch,<ref>{{cite web|title=Brazil progress as Chile pay penalty |url=http://www.espnfc.com/fifa-world-cup/match/383254/brazil-chile/report |publisher=ESPN |access-date=28 June 2014 }}</ref> FIFA later confirmed that the goal was scored by Luiz, not Jara.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nydailynews.com/sports/soccer/fifa-confirms-brazil-goal-scored-david-luiz-article-1.1848397|title=FIFA confirms Brazil's goal vs. Chile in Round of 16 was scored by David Luiz, not ruled an own goal by Gonzalo Jara|work=New York Daily News|date=29 June 2014}}</ref> Chile equalised when [[Hulk (footballer)|Hulk]] lost possession after a throw-in in his own half; [[Eduardo Vargas]] stole the ball and passed to [[Alexis Sánchez]] to score.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/29/brazil-chile-world-cup-2014-last-16-match-report|title=Brazil struggle to recover from emotional shootout win over Chile|date=29 June 2014 |work=Guardian|access-date=30 October 2014}}</ref>

In the second half, Hulk's goal was disallowed as [[Howard Webb]] adjudged that the player used his arm in bringing down the ball and gave him a yellow card instead.<ref>{{cite web|title=Hulk revels in supporting act |url=http://www.espnfc.com/team/brazil/205/blog/post/1920246/hulk-revels-in-supporting-act |publisher=ESPN |first=John |last=Brewin |access-date=28 June 2014 }}</ref> Chances were few after that with Brazil dominating possession and Hulk forcing [[Claudio Bravo]] into a decent save, and the match went to extra time. The best chance of extra time came in the last seconds as [[Mauricio Pinilla]]'s shot hit the crossbar.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/27952759|title=Brazil 1 Chile 1 (Brazil win 3–2 on penalties)|publisher=BBC Sport|date=28 June 2014}}</ref> In the resulting penalty shoot-out, the score was tied 2–2 after four rounds, with Brazil goalkeeper [[Júlio César (football goalkeeper, born 1979)|Júlio César]] saving from Pinilla and Sánchez, while [[Willian (footballer, born 1988)|Willian]] missed and Hulk's shot was saved by Bravo. In the fifth round, [[Neymar]] scored his penalty, meaning Chile had to score, but Jara's shot hit the inside of the post.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=255951/match=300186487/match-report.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140628195726/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=255951/match=300186487/match-report.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=28 June 2014 |title=Brazil through as Chile pay the penalty |publisher=FIFA |access-date=28 June 2014 }}</ref> Brazil advanced to the quarter-finals to face [[#Brazil vs Colombia|Colombia]].

The result meant that in all four World Cups where Chile qualified for the knockout stage, they were eliminated by Brazil.

