===Colombia vs Uruguay===
The two teams had met in 38 previous matches,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/statisticsandrecords/headtohead/team1=col/team2=uru/code=-1/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140708194845/http://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/statisticsandrecords/headtohead/team1=col/team2=uru/code=-1/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=8 July 2014|title=Colombia – Uruguay|publisher=FIFA |access-date=28 June 2014 }}</ref> including in the [[1962 FIFA World Cup]] group stage, won 2–1 by Uruguay. Their most recent meetings were in the [[2014 FIFA World Cup qualification (CONMEBOL)|2014 FIFA World Cup qualifiers]], with both teams winning at home, Colombia winning [[2014 FIFA World Cup qualification (CONMEBOL)#Colombia v Uruguay|4–0]] and Uruguay winning [[2014 FIFA World Cup qualification (CONMEBOL)#Uruguay v Colombia|2–0]]. Uruguayan striker [[Luis Suárez]] was not in the line-up because of a nine-game ban imposed by FIFA due to a [[List of 2014 FIFA World Cup controversies#Uruguay vs Italy|biting incident]] involving Italian defender [[Giorgio Chiellini]] during their [[2014 FIFA World Cup Group D#Italy vs Uruguay|final group stage match]].<ref name="Suarez suspension">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2014/m=6/news=luis-suarez-suspended-for-nine-matches-and-banned-for-four-months-from-2386354.html |title=Luis Suárez suspended for nine matches and banned for four months from any football-related activity |publisher=FIFA |date=26 June 2014 |access-date=26 June 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140703172739/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y%3D2014/m%3D6/news%3Dluis-suarez-suspended-for-nine-matches-and-banned-for-four-months-from-2386354.html |archive-date=3 July 2014 }}</ref> This was the first match of Uruguay in [[Maracanã Stadium]] in a World Cup since the historical [[Uruguay v Brazil (1950 FIFA World Cup)|Maracanazo]].

Colombia won 2–0 with both goals from [[James Rodríguez]], the first in the 28th minute, where he controlled [[Abel Aguilar]]'s headed ball on his chest before volleying left-footed from 25 yards out with the ball going in off the underside of the crossbar,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/29/colombia-uruguay-james-rodriguez|title=Colombia's James Rodríguez seeks top honours and sends warning to Brazil|date=29 June 2014 |work=Guardian|access-date=30 October 2014}}</ref> which won the [[FIFA Puskás Award#2014|2014 FIFA Puskás Award]] later in the year.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/ballon-dor/puskasaward/02/50/58/03/puskas_votes_1_neutral.pdf |title=FIFA Puskás award 2014 – results |publisher=FIFA |access-date=9 February 2015 |archive-date=4 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304114640/http://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/ballon-dor/puskasaward/02/50/58/03/puskas_votes_1_neutral.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref>
The second goal, in the 50th minute, was a close-range shot from six yards out after receiving the ball from a header by [[Juan Cuadrado]] on the right.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/27952760 |title=Colombia 2 Uruguay 0 |date=29 June 2014 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=3 July 2014 }}</ref>

Colombia progressed through to the quarter-finals for the first time in their history, where they would face [[#Brazil vs Colombia|Brazil]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/28/colombia-uruguay-world-cup-2014-last-16-match-report |title=James Rodríguez sizzle ends Uruguay's World Cup saga |date=29 June 2014 |work=Guardian |access-date=3 July 2014 }}</ref>

