===Germany vs Algeria===
The two teams had met in two previous matches,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/statisticsandrecords/headtohead/team1=ger/team2=alg/code=-1/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140708194902/http://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/statisticsandrecords/headtohead/team1=ger/team2=alg/code=-1/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=8 July 2014 |title=Germany – Algeria |publisher=FIFA |access-date=28 June 2014 }}</ref> including in the [[1982 FIFA World Cup]] group stage, where Algeria defeated West Germany 2–1. This match was Algeria's first ever in the FIFA World Cup knockout stage.

After a goalless 90 minutes when an Algerian goal was disallowed, Germany opened the scoring two minutes into extra time, when half-time substitute [[André Schürrle]] scored with a left foot back-heel from [[Thomas Müller]]'s cross from the left.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/30/germany-algeria-world-cup-last-16-match-report|title=Germany edge past Algeria as Schürrle and Özil end stalemate in extra time|date=30 June 2014 |work=Guardian|access-date=30 October 2014}}</ref>
Germany extended the lead in the 119th minute when [[Mesut Özil]] converted with his left foot high into the net after Schürrle's shot was blocked on the line, but Algeria pulled one back in injury time when substitute [[Abdelmoumene Djabou]] scored with his left foot from six yards after a cross from the right by [[Sofiane Feghouli]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/27969924|title=Germany 2 Algeria 1|publisher=BBC Sport|date=30 June 2014}}</ref>

Germany advanced to the quarter-finals to face [[#Germany vs France|France]], continuing their streak of reaching the last eight in every World Cup since 1954.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2115412-germany-have-no-need-to-panic-ahead-of-world-cup-quarter-final-with-france|title=Germany Have No Need to Panic Ahead of World Cup Quarter-Final with France|work=Bleacher Report|date=1 July 2014}}</ref> Djabou's goal, timed at 120 minutes and 51 seconds, was the latest goal in World Cup history, surpassing [[Alessandro Del Piero]]'s goal against Germany in 2006, timed at 120 minutes and 13 seconds, until [[Youri Tielemans]]'s penalty goal against [[Senegal national football team|Senegal]] in 2026, timed at 124 minutes and 44 seconds.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://it.eurosport.yahoo.com/blog/the-rio-report-it/djabou-cancella-record-alex-piero-021956670.html|title=Djabou cancella il record di Alex Del Piero|language=it|publisher=Yahoo! Sport Italia|date=1 July 2014|access-date=8 July 2014|archive-date=15 July 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140715000936/https://it.eurosport.yahoo.com/blog/the-rio-report-it/djabou-cancella-record-alex-piero-021956670.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

