==Semi-finals==
===Brazil vs Germany===
{{main|Brazil v Germany (2014 FIFA World Cup)}}
[[File:Brazil vs Germany, in Belo Horizonte 05.jpg|thumb|right|250px|[[Miroslav Klose]] (center) celebrating with teammates after scoring the second goal for Germany.]]
The two teams had met in 21 previous matches, including in the [[2002 FIFA World Cup Final|final]] of the [[2002 FIFA World Cup]] (their only previous encounter in the tournament's history), won by Brazil 2–0.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/statisticsandrecords/headtohead/team1=bra/team2=ger/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070613063056/http://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/statisticsandrecords/headtohead/team1=BRA/team2=GER/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=13 June 2007|title=Brazil – Germany |publisher=FIFA |access-date=7 July 2014 }}</ref>

While Germany kept the same starting line-up as their quarter-final against France, Brazil made two changes: defender and captain [[Thiago Silva]] was suspended for the match due to accumulation of yellow cards,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/07/world-cup-2014-thiago-silva-brazil-germany|title=Brazil appeal against Thiago Silva's World Cup semi-final ban rejected|work=[[The Guardian]]|date=7 July 2014}}</ref> and was replaced by [[Dante (footballer)|Dante]], while forward [[Neymar]] was sidelined after suffering a [[Bone fracture|fractured]] [[Vertebra (anatomy)|vertebra]] in the quarter-final against Colombia,<ref name="Neymar out for the tournament" /> and was replaced by [[Bernard (footballer)|Bernard]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lavanguardia.com/deportes/20140708/54411712232/bernard-y-dante-por-neymar-y-thiago-silva-low-apuesta-por-klose.html|title=Bernard y Dante por Neymar y Thiago Silva; Low apuesta por Klose|publisher=La Vanguardia|date=8 July 2014}}</ref> Germany took the lead in the 11th minute, [[Thomas Müller]] volleying in [[Toni Kroos]]'s corner. [[Miroslav Klose]] scored Germany's second goal, after a passing move saw him set up by Kroos, his first shot was saved by Brazil goalkeeper [[Júlio César (football goalkeeper, born 1979)|Júlio César]], but he slotted in the rebound. Kroos then scored two quick goals in succession, the first one after [[Philipp Lahm]]'s cross was missed by Müller, the second one after stealing the ball from [[Fernandinho (footballer, born May 1985)|Fernandinho]] and playing a quick one-two with [[Sami Khedira]]. Khedira then scored Germany's fourth goal in six minutes, after exchanging passes with [[Mesut Özil]], to give Germany the 5–0 half-time lead. Substitute [[André Schürrle]] added two more goals in the second half, first slotting in from Lahm's cross, then scoring via the crossbar after a cut-back from Müller. Brazil scored a consolation goal in the 90th minute, as [[Oscar (footballer, born 1991)|Oscar]] received a pass from [[Marcelo (footballer, born 1988)|Marcelo]], dribbled inside and scored.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/28102403|title=Brazil 1 Germany 7|publisher=BBC Sport|date=8 July 2014}}</ref> Germany reached their eighth World Cup [[2014 FIFA World Cup Final|final]], a record by any nation, where they would face [[#Final|Argentina]], while Brazil had to settle for the [[#Match for third place|match for third place against the Netherlands]].

The game equalled Brazil's biggest margin of defeat, a [[1920 South American Championship#Matches|6–0 loss to Uruguay in 1920]], and it broke a 62-match home unbeaten streak in competitive matches going back to the [[1975 Copa América#Final round|1975 Copa América]], where they lost 3–1 to Peru in the same exact stadium of this match.<ref name="New York Times match summary">{{cite news|last1=Klein|first1=Jeff|title=World Cup 2014: Germany Defeats Brazil, 7–1|url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2014/07/08/sports/worldcup/world-cup-brazil-vs-germany.html|access-date=8 July 2014|work=New York Times}}</ref> The match also broke many [[Brazil v Germany (2014 FIFA World Cup)#Records|World Cup records]]: It was Brazil's biggest World Cup defeat (eclipsing their [[1998 FIFA World Cup Final|1998 final]] loss to France), the biggest defeat by a World Cup host nation (previous record was by three goals), and the biggest margin of victory in a World Cup semi-final (three previous semi-finals ended in 6–1 scorelines).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/chrissmith/2014/07/08/germany-embarrasses-brazil-in-worst-loss-on-home-soil-in-world-cup-history/|title=Germany Embarrasses Brazil In Worst Loss On Home Soil In World Cup History|work=Forbes|date=8 July 2014}}</ref>

Klose's goal was his 16th at World Cup finals tournaments, allowing him to overtake Brazil's [[Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)|Ronaldo]] as the [[FIFA World Cup top goalscorers|tournament's all-time top scorer]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-world-ger-klose-goals/miroslav-kloses-world-cup-goals-idUSKBN0FD2L520140709|title=Miroslav Klose's World Cup goals|newspaper=Reuters|date=9 July 2014}}</ref> Germany's seven goals took their total tally in World Cup history to 223, surpassing Brazil's 221 goals to first place overall.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2014/m=7/news=the-mineirazo-in-numbers-2402146.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140709023442/http://www.fifa.com//worldcup/news/y=2014/m=7/news=the-mineirazo-in-numbers-2402146.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=9 July 2014|title=The Mineirazo in numbers|publisher=FIFA |date=9 July 2014}}</ref>

