==Final==
{{Main|2014 FIFA World Cup final}}
[[File:2014 FIFA World Cup Final, Germany vs Argentina lineup.jpeg|thumb|right|Germany–Argentina line-up before kick-off]]
The two teams had met in 20 previous matches, including six times in the FIFA World Cup: [[1958 FIFA World Cup|1958]], group stage: West Germany 3–1 Argentina; [[1966 FIFA World Cup|1966]], group stage: West Germany 0–0 Argentina; [[1986 FIFA World Cup|1986]], [[1986 FIFA World Cup Final|final]]: West Germany 2–3 Argentina; [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]], [[1990 FIFA World Cup Final|final]]: West Germany 1–0 Argentina; [[2006 FIFA World Cup|2006]], [[2006 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Germany vs Argentina|quarter-finals]]: Germany 1–1 Argentina (after extra time, Germany won 4–2 on penalties); [[2010 FIFA World Cup]], [[2010 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Argentina vs Germany|quarter-finals]]: Germany 4–0 Argentina.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/statisticsandrecords/headtohead/team1=ger/team2=arg/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100530074813/http://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/statisticsandrecords/headtohead/team1=ger/team2=arg/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=30 May 2010|title=Germany – Argentina |publisher=FIFA |access-date=11 July 2014 }}</ref> Their third final meeting is the most ever in World Cup history, and also tied the record for the most frequent fixture in tournament history, as Brazil vs Sweden and Germany vs Serbia (including Yugoslavia) had also been played seven times in the World Cup.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://sunnewsonline.com/new/?p=71900|location=Lagos, Nigeria|title=WORLD CUP FINAL: Argentina hopes for third win as Germany wants fourth|date=13 July 2014|newspaper=[[The Sun (Nigeria)|The Sun]]}}</ref>

Both teams initially named unchanged starting line-ups from their semi-finals,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sbnation.com/soccer/2014/7/13/5895799/germany-argentina-world-cup-2014-lineups|title=World Cup 2014 final lineups: Both Germany and Argentina unchanged|publisher=SB Nation|date=13 July 2014}}</ref> but Germany had to make a late change as [[Sami Khedira]] injured his calf and was replaced by [[Christoph Kramer]], who was himself substituted in the first half by [[André Schürrle]] after a blow to his head.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sbnation.com/soccer/2014/7/13/5895921/christoph-kramer-replaces-sami-khedira-in-germany-lineup|title=Christoph Kramer replaces Sami Khedira in Germany lineup|publisher=SB Nation|date=13 July 2014}}</ref> In the first half, [[Gonzalo Higuaín]] shot wide after a [[Toni Kroos]]'s misplaced header fell to him, and also had a goal disallowed for offside. Later, [[Jérôme Boateng]] cleared off the line after a [[Lionel Messi]] run, and [[Benedikt Höwedes]]'s header hit the post. In the second half, Argentina's best chance fell to Messi, whose shot went just wide, while [[Toni Kroos]] also had a chance for Germany but could not convert. For the third World Cup final in a row, the match went to extra time. In the first half, Schürrle had a shot saved by [[Sergio Romero]], while [[Rodrigo Palacio]]'s lobbed shot was also unsuccessful. Germany scored the only goal in the second half, as Schürrle ran down the left wing and crossed for substitute [[Mario Götze]], who controlled the ball on his chest and volleyed past Romero.<ref>{{cite web|date=13 July 2014|title=Germany 1-0 Argentina|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/28181689|publisher=BBC Sport}}</ref>

Germany won their fourth World Cup title, and their first title since [[German reunification]] (they won as West Germany in [[1954 FIFA World Cup|1954]], [[1974 FIFA World Cup|1974]], and [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]]). It was the first time a European team won the tournament in the Americas. For the third tournament in a row, the World Cup was won by a European team, first time for any confederation. Argentina finished as the runners-up for the third time in their history.

A total of 171 goals were scored in the tournament, a joint record along with the [[1998 FIFA World Cup]]. Götze's goal was the 32nd by a substitute, a World Cup record. Including the final, a total of eight matches went to extra time, a joint record along with the [[1990 FIFA World Cup]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.fourfourtwo.com/sg/features/pass-move-and-take-your-chances-how-stats-zone-saw-germany-1-0-argentina|title=Pass, move and take your chances: how Stats Zone saw Germany 1–0 Argentina|work=FourFourTwo|date=13 July 2014|access-date=13 July 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160619050421/http://www.fourfourtwo.com/sg/features/pass-move-and-take-your-chances-how-stats-zone-saw-germany-1-0-argentina|archive-date=19 June 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref>

